---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import SourcedBlock from "../../../components/SourcedBlock.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import DataTable from "../../../components/story/DataTable.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import { publishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import { event, primary, poll, ballotRows, timeline, next } from "../../../data/election-administration-2026-10-23-doj-sues-to-block-alaska-senate-certification.mjs";
const state = publishedState().events.get("event-election-administration-2026-10-23-doj-sues-to-block-alaska-senate-certification");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-election-administration-2026-10-23-doj-sues-to-block-alaska-senate-certification");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
const ballot = ballotRows.map((r) => [r.name, r.label || "nothing"]);
const steps = [
  { text: "Sen. Dan S. Sullivan loses the election.", c: "suit-condition" },
  { text: "Had every vote for Daniel J. Sullivan been cast for the senator, with the ranked-choice rounds played out on that basis, the senator would have won.", c: "suit-condition" },
  { text: "For a result where both hold, the suit asks the court to declare that certifying such a result would violate the state's duties under the voter-registration law, and to keep the state from certifying it pending an investigation of whether votes for Daniel J. Sullivan were meant for the senator.", c: "suit-relief" },
];
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/election-administration/">Election administration</a><span>October 23, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Alaska Senate race &middot; October 23, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#two-sullivans">Two Sullivans on one ballot</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-got-here">How it got here</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">Does the law allow it?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Justice Department sued Alaska and its election officials in federal court on Oct. 22.<Cite c="suit-filed" /> It asks a judge to stop the state from certifying its U.S. Senate result in one situation: if Republican Sen. Dan S. Sullivan loses, but would have won had every vote for a second candidate, Daniel J. Sullivan, been cast for him.<Cite c="suit-condition" /> Then the department wants the result held until an investigation finds whether votes for Daniel J. Sullivan were meant for the senator.<Cite c="suit-relief" /> It rests the suit on a 1993 voter-registration law.<Cite c="doj-legal-basis" /><Cite c="nvra-purposes-text" /> The next day President Trump said he had ordered the suit. "I'm the one that said 'do it,'" he told reporters.<Cite c="trump-ordered" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The senator's Democratic opponent is Mary Peltola.<Cite c="ballot-listing" /> The complaint says many polls put the race within one or two points.<Cite c="polls-close" /> In the August primary Daniel J. Sullivan won {primary.danielJ}% of the vote, to Peltola's {primary.peltola}% and the senator's {primary.senator}%.<Cite c="primary-shares" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The case is before Judge Sharon Gleason, and the department has made no emergency request.<Cite c="judge-no-motion" /> Alaska's Department of Law, which represents the state's election officials, says it is reviewing the complaint.<Cite c="alaska-no-comment" /> Early voting is already under way for the Nov. 3 election.<Cite c="early-voting-underway" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>"If Republican Loses"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Much of the first coverage described the suit as blocking certification if Sullivan loses, if a Democrat wins, or if Daniel J. Sullivan stays on the ballot, or as blocking the vote.<Cite c="coverage-if-loses" /> The complaint sets two conditions, and both must hold: the senator loses, and he would have won had all of Daniel J. Sullivan's votes gone to him, with the ranked-choice count worked through again on that basis.<Cite c="suit-condition" /> A loss that still stands in that hypothetical count does not meet the test, and what the suit asks to stop is certification, pending an investigation.<Cite c="suit-condition" /><Cite c="suit-relief" /> CBS's report gave both conditions in its body, and the Washington Post's headline and the Times's opening said the suit applies if the incumbent "narrowly loses." That is how Derek Muller of Notre Dame reads it: he said the suit would likely only kick in if Republicans narrowly lost.<Cite c="coverage-got-it" /><Cite c="muller-no-duty" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>What happens if the test is met</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The test counts every vote for Daniel J. Sullivan as the senator's, whatever the voter meant.<Cite c="suit-condition" /> Meeting it would not make the senator the winner. It would keep Alaska from certifying while an investigation looks into whether those votes were meant for him, and the department also asks for any further procedures the court finds necessary to determine what the result would have been without Daniel J. Sullivan on the ballot.<Cite c="suit-relief" /><Cite c="suit-further-relief" /> The complaint does not say how that investigation would work, when votes are supposed to be confidential.<Cite c="suit-no-method" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>How close it would have to be</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">One poll has run a narrower version of the test. Data for Progress, surveying 694 likely voters from Oct. 8 to 16, found Peltola ahead {poll.finalPeltola}% to {poll.finalSenator}% after a simulated ranked-choice count. When the ballots of Daniel J. Sullivan's voters that had run out of ranked candidates, the exhausted ballots, were given to the senator, the race was tied. The poll's margin of error is 4 points.<Cite c="dfp-simulation" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="two-sullivans">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Two Sullivans on one ballot</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Alaska's ballot lets voters rank as many candidates as they like, so a voter can rank both Sullivans.<Cite c="rank-both" /> The state elections division decided how the two would appear. Daniel J. Sullivan filed as a Republican, but the division printed him with no party, on the August primary ballot and again on November's.<Cite c="division-chose-listing" /> Elections director Carol Beecher said the aim was to "maximize differentiation" so voters would not confuse the two.<Cite c="listing-reason" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The Senate race as the November ballot prints it</strong></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Name", "Printed beside it"]} rows={ballot} ariaLabel="The four Senate candidates on Alaska's November ballot and the label printed beside each name" />
          <p class="chart-source">From the sample ballot filed with the complaint.<Cite c="ballot-listing" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The department says the listing is not enough. Its complaint alleges that some of the senator's supporters will likely vote for Daniel J. Sullivan by mistake, and that others will be confused about how to rank "the three Republican candidates" and may not rank the senator at all.<Cite c="doj-confusion-claim" /> The primary used the same listing, and there the senator won {primary.senatorVotes} votes to Daniel J. Sullivan's {primary.danielJVotes}.<Cite c="division-chose-listing" /><Cite c="primary-votes" /> An Anchorage Press analysis of those returns found his support rose where Peltola ran strongest, a sign that many of his votes came from her side; it could not rule out that some conservatives picked him by mistake.<Cite c="primary-pattern" /> The senator's own campaign read the primary the same way: "Mary Peltola's voters put Daniel J. Sullivan Jr. on the November ballot."<Cite c="sullivan-camp-peltola-voters" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The test, and what meeting it would bring</strong></figcaption>
          <ol class="decision-timeline" style="padding-left:0;list-style:none" aria-label="The three steps of the test the complaint asks the court to apply">
            {steps.map((s, i) => <li class="dt-row" data-layer="fact"><span class="dt-date">{["Condition 1", "Condition 2", "Then"][i]}</span><span class="dt-body">{s.text}<Cite c={s.c} /></span></li>)}
          </ol>
          <p class="chart-source">From the complaint's definition of a result that may be affected by confusion and the relief it seeks.<Cite c="suit-condition" /><Cite c="suit-relief" /></p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="how-it-got-here">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">How it got here</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The department says it acted because Alaska's own elections division had found that Daniel J. Sullivan's candidacy was meant to mislead voters, and a spokesperson called it a "frivolous candidacy."<Cite c="doj-reason" /> That finding did not survive in Alaska's courts. A state judge ruled that the division had judged him by a good-faith test found nowhere in Alaska's constitution or election laws, and that the evidence did not show he sought to confuse voters. The Alaska Supreme Court affirmed.<Cite c="courts-restored" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The complaint repeats the charge. It says Daniel J. Sullivan ran to mislead the senator's supporters and that his candidacy's sole purpose is to help Peltola.<Cite c="doj-claims-purpose" /> Trump calls him "the fake."<Cite c="trump-fake" /> Daniel J. Sullivan says he has every right to run, and the suit does not name him as a defendant.<Cite c="daniel-j-says" /><Cite c="not-defendant" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The suit was not the first federal move against his candidacy: in August, a source told Reuters, a federal grand jury had issued subpoenas in a Justice Department probe of it.<Cite c="subpoenas-august" /> Trump's stated aim was different from the suit's. On Oct. 21 he said he was looking at "powerful legal options" to take Daniel J. Sullivan off the ballot. The suit, filed a day later, asks instead for a hold on certification if the test above is met.<Cite c="trump-legal-options" /><Cite c="suit-filed" /><Cite c="suit-condition" /><Cite c="suit-relief" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the ballot fight to the suit, 2026</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} heading="What happened" />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">This fall the administration has also gone to court over other parts of the midterms. A week before the Alaska suit the department sued California, asking a court to bar the state from counting ballots from about 20,000 voters it says are likely noncitizens.<Cite c="california-suit" /> In September, the Supreme Court said the administration's <a href="/events/mail-voting/2026-09-14-court-denies-stay/">restrictions on mail ballots</a> would most likely lose in court, at least for this year's election.<Cite c="mail-ballot-rule" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Does the law allow it?</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The suit rests on one clause of the National Voter Registration Act of 1993, which lists protecting "the integrity of the electoral process" among the law's purposes, and on the law's grant of power to the Attorney General to sue for relief "necessary to carry out" it.<Cite c="doj-legal-basis" /><Cite c="nvra-20510-text" /> The complaint is signed by Associate Attorney General Stanley Woodward.<Cite c="suit-signed-woodward" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The clause is the third of four purposes the law lists. The other three are to increase the number of eligible citizens who register, to let governments carry out the law in ways that enhance eligible citizens' participation as voters, and to keep voter rolls accurate.<Cite c="nvra-purposes-text" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="National Voter Registration Act" when="1993" where="52 U.S.C. § 20501(b)(3)" label="The clause the suit rests on">
          <p>"to protect the integrity of the electoral process"<Cite c="nvra-purposes-text" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The department's reading</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The department asks the court to declare that certifying a result that meets its test would violate Alaska's duties under that law.<Cite c="suit-relief" /> Its main Supreme Court precedent, it concedes, was about who gets on the ballot, not how votes are counted.<Cite c="bullock-concession" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The critics' reading</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Election lawyers say the clause gives no such power. Derek Muller of Notre Dame says it places no duties on a state beyond those the law spells out, and calls the argument "one of the worst ones I've ever seen."<Cite c="muller-no-duty" /> Vanita Gupta, a former associate attorney general, says no federal statute authorizes the relief the department seeks.<Cite c="gupta-no-statute" /> In April, in a case about voter records, the federal appeals court whose rulings bind courts in Alaska called that section the law's "Preamble."<Cite c="ca9-preamble" /> Michael Morley of Florida State says the worry about a sham candidate is real, but that this suit is frivolous and will be quickly dismissed.<Cite c="morley-real-concern" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The candidates and Alaska's other senator</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Sen. Lisa Murkowski, Alaska's other Republican senator, says the state already handled the problem by clearly distinguishing the candidates on the ballot, and that the department should never have filed the suit.<Cite c="murkowski" /> Peltola says Sullivan is "using his DC allies to try to keep himself in power," and her campaign calls the suit "a despicable political threat to overturn an election."<Cite c="peltola-allies" /><Cite c="peltola-camp-overturn" /> The senator's campaign says it had "no prior knowledge of this lawsuit and no role in it."<Cite c="sullivan-camp-no-role" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Beyond the headlines that dropped the second condition, one report got the test itself wrong, turning it into a sum of votes. UPI wrote that the department wants certification delayed if "the combination of votes for the two men named Dan Sullivan would surpass Peltola's number of votes." The complaint does not add the two men's votes against Peltola's. It redoes the ranked-choice count with Daniel J. Sullivan's votes given to the senator and asks whether the senator would then win.<Cite c="upi-combination" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Three smaller errors do not change the account of the suit. CBS gave the filing as Friday's; it was Thursday, a slip the Times also made and corrected.<Cite c="cbs-friday" /> CNBC put the primary on Aug. 28 and the senator's share at 41.1%; the primary was Aug. 18 and his share 41.4%, and Aug. 28 is the date the complaint itself gives.<Cite c="cnbc-aug28" /> UPI called it a Republican primary; it was open to every candidate, with the top four advancing.<Cite c="upi-republican-primary" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Muller says a wide Democratic win or a narrow Republican one could make it moot, and that otherwise the court will likely dismiss it.<Cite c="moot-scenarios" /> Peltola's campaign has suggested it may join the case.<Cite c="peltola-may-intervene" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>After the Nov. 3 election</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={next} heading="Scheduled" />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Morley says that if the senator loses by less than Daniel J. Sullivan's vote, his recourse is an election contest in the U.S. Senate, which judges its own members' elections.<Cite c="senate-judge" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
